Task 2 — bind the wheelhouse to its build Python

Why:
- a SearXNG wheelhouse is valid only for the exact Python implementation, minor and platform used to build it;
- interrupted publication must recover the last complete bundle without accepting mixed metadata.

Implementation:
- RuntimeIdentity is the single authority joining the preparation stamp and strict runtime manifest;
- manifests reject unknown or duplicate fields, unsafe files and inconsistent stamp/runtime hashes;
- metadata is created exclusively and regular-file checks reject symlinks before any target can be overwritten;
- current journals retain the complete identity, while legacy journals retain their original stamp only for bounded backward recovery;
- the transaction state matrix validates NEW against the recorded target identity and OLD against its own recoverable identity before commit or rollback;
- transaction filesystem and journal responsibilities were extracted so every production file remains below 230 lines.

Review history:
- review found rollback loss when the old bundle had another identity, stamp/runtime divergence, and metadata symlink overwrite;
- it also found an over-limit transaction module and duplicated identity logic;
- all findings were corrected before closure.

Evidence:
- 43/43 targeted Python tests pass;
- Ruff and git diff --check pass;
- direct reproductions confirm divergent identities are rejected, current crash states roll back safely, and legacy post-backup states recover;
- mutation: validating OLD against the new identity makes four crash-matrix tests fail;
- mutation: weakening lowercase hexadecimal stamp validation makes test_legacy_bundle_requires_lowercase_hex_stamps fail.

Limits:
- no live pip download is claimed in this task;
- GUI runtime discovery and offline environment construction belong to Tasks 3 and 4.
